Title:
Introducing Winbindex – the Windows Binaries Index

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post introduces Winbindex, a web index of Windows binaries found in Windows Update packages, with metadata and direct download links for many PE files (exe/dll/sys) from Microsoft servers. It addresses the pain of collecting many historical versions of a specific system binary (e.g., to pinpoint when a bug was fixed) without downloading large ISOs or manually installing updates. The core technique is extracting SHA256 hashes from update package manifests, using VirusTotal to derive PE timestamp and image size, then constructing Microsoft Symbol Server URLs to fetch the corresponding binaries. It also discusses complications like PSFX forward/reverse differentials in modern updates and collisions caused by Windows 10’s reproducible-build “pseudo-timestamps.” The result is a searchable dataset useful for vulnerability research, reverse engineering, and patch diffing, with caveats about symbol server collisions sometimes returning non-production/unsigned builds.

Technical Focus:
- Windows Update package structure (MSU/CAB, assemblies, manifests)
- PSFX forward/reverse differentials and patch distribution
- PE metadata derivation (timestamp, section layout → image size)
- Microsoft Symbol Server path construction and binary retrieval
- VirusTotal hash-based enrichment and rate-limiting evasion via Tor
- Reproducible builds and timestamp/image-size collision analysis

Use Cases:
- Rapidly obtain multiple historical versions of a Windows driver/DLL for diffing
- Identify which KB/update introduced a fix or regression
- Support reverse engineering and vulnerability research on Windows components
- Validate file hashes/metadata across update releases and investigate anomalies
- Build automation pipelines for binary collection from Microsoft infrastructure

Keywords:
Winbindex, Windows Update Catalog, MSU, CAB, assembly manifest, SHA256, VirusTotal API, Microsoft Symbol Server, msdl.microsoft.com, PE timestamp, image size, section table, PSFX, forward differential, reverse differential, reproducible builds, patch diffing, driver analysis, srv2.sys, Tor, PyMultitor